---
// The project card (specs/pages/projects-index.md §2.1-2.2), rendered on / and
// on /projects/. Lives inside CardGrid.astro, which owns the track geometry.
//
// This component is where §2.2's disclosure test stops being a convention.
// The test — "no two cards with visually identical affordances produce
// different navigation outcomes" — is a property of the card, so there has to
// be exactly one card; src/styles/projects.css said so and enforced what a
// stylesheet can enforce, which is the treatment. The markup, the rel, the
// aria wiring and the destination lookup were duplicated in both templates
// under a comment asking the next editor to keep them byte-identical. That
// comment was the enforcement mechanism. Here the markup and the policy are
// one file, so a second card that looks the same and behaves differently is
// not a thing a page can write by accident.
//
// The card is not a link; only its title is. The prototype's card-wide hover
// is dropped for that reason — it would advertise an affordance that is not
// there.
//
// ── the hairline ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The prototype's card idiom (`.cards { gap: 1px; background: var(--border) }`
// over filled cards) is deliberately not used: under auto-fill the border
// background paints the *empty* tracks too, producing the layout-with-holes
// §2.3 rejects. The hairline moves onto the card's own block-end edge instead.
// `border-be border-be-border` is the logical pair — the block axis does not
// flip under dir="rtl", but naming it logically keeps the whole device on one
// vocabulary.
//
// ── title, description and host, and why they are not shared ───────────────
//
// These three repeat declarations that the home page's .row-* rules carry, and
// that entry-list.css's .entry-title carries again. That repetition is the
// house posture, stated at projects.css:46-49: each archetype device owns its
// own title, description and meta treatment, and they are not to be
// consolidated. Extraction does not change it. Sharing ONE card between two
// pages is the single-definition-point property the specs ask for; sharing a
// title treatment between three unrelated devices is coupling, and the specs
// forbid it explicitly.
//
// ── the title's underline, which is three utilities and has to be ──────────
//
// `.card-title` set `text-decoration: none` and `.card-title:hover` set
// `text-decoration: underline`. Both are SHORTHANDS, and the shorthand also
// resets decoration colour to currentcolor and thickness to auto. The
// `no-underline` and `underline` utilities set the line and nothing else, so
// on their own the rule would keep global.css's 1px --border-strong decoration
// instead of a currentcolor hairline at the browser's own thickness.
// `decoration-current decoration-auto` restores what the shorthand reset; they
// sit unprefixed because the resting shorthand reset them too, where the line
// is none and they cannot be seen. This exact omission cost 61 pixels in this
// migration's pilot, on one page, and it is the reason the pilot was run.
//
// `[&:hover]:` and never `hover:`: Tailwind v4 wraps `hover:` in
// `@media (hover: hover)`, dropping the state on touch devices — where no
// screenshot can catch it. The two hover utilities out-specify the resting
// ones (`.a:hover` over `.a`), so the branch resolves on specificity and not
// on the emit order, which is not ours to control.
//
// ── the destination ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Where a card links, and whether it discloses a host, is `destination()` in
// src/lib/projects — one implementation, because that function is the rule
// (§2.2, and its own header comment). The card calls it rather than taking
// href and host as props, so there is no call site left that could ask it
// differently, or pass a hand-computed answer, or forget to ask.
//
// The host is bound with aria-describedby rather than merely set beside the
// title, so it reaches someone navigating by links: §2.2's test is about what
// a visitor can tell BEFORE the click, and a span outside the anchor tells
// only sighted ones. `rel="noopener"` and the description are set from the
// same condition on purpose — an outbound card has both, an inward card has
// neither.
import type { CollectionEntry } from 'astro:content';
import StackChips from './StackChips.astro';
import { destination } from '../lib/projects';

interface Props {
  entry: CollectionEntry<'projects'>;
}

const { entry } = Astro.props;
const { href, host } = destination(entry);
const hostId = `card-host-${entry.id}`;
---

<li class="pbs-5 pbe-5 border-be border-be-border">
  <a
    class="inline-block text-size-lede font-bold text-text-strong no-underline decoration-current decoration-auto [&:hover]:underline [&:hover]:underline-offset-[0.2em]"
    href={href}
    rel={host === null ? undefined : 'noopener'}
    aria-describedby={host === null ? undefined : hostId}
  >
    {entry.data.title}
  </a>

  <p class="mbs-1.5 text-size-meta text-muted">{entry.data.description}</p>

  {/* The outbound card names where it goes; the inward card has nothing extra
      to disclose (§2.2). */}
  {
    host !== null && (
      <span class="block mbs-2 font-mono text-size-eyebrow text-muted" id={hostId}>
        {host}
      </span>
    )
  }

  {/* Chip placement is the caller's (StackChips.astro): mbs-3 is the 12px that
      projects.css's `.card .stack-chips` rule used to reach in and set. */}
  <StackChips stack={entry.data.stack} class="mbs-3" />
</li>
